            <ArticleTitle>Current trends in the management of acute appendicitis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>121-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s11845-008-0116-4</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">While laparoscopic appendectomy (LA) has become established in the diagnosis and treatment of acute appendicitis, its utilisation compared to open appendectomy (OA) is variable.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To compare the utilisation and outcome of laparoscopic (LA) versus OA in an Irish regional hospital setting.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHOD" NlmCategory="METHODS">Retrospective review of OA and LA performed from 2003 to 2005.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Intention-to-treat analysis of 787 patients in this study revealed that 149 patients (19%) had LA and 638 patients (81%) had OA. Consultants were significantly more likely than trainees to undertake a LA (P &lt; 0.0001). Twenty-two complications (2.8%) were recorded in the post-operative period. The overall negative appendectomy rate by histopathology was 17% with no significant difference between the rate in the LA group (19%) and the OA (17%) group.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Mean length of stay and complication rate were comparable between the LA and OA groups.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
